An if statement allows your code to make decisions. It checks a condition, which is a comparison that results in either true or false. If the condition is true, the code inside the if block runs. If it is false, the code inside the else block runs instead.

To compare two values for equality, use the strict equality operator, which is three equals signs (===). For example, if you have a variable named score and want to check if it equals 100, you write score === 100.

Do not confuse this with the single equals sign (=). The single equals sign is for assignment, which means you are giving a value to a variable, not checking if two things are the same.

For example:
const level = 5;
if (level === 5) {
  console.log("Welcome");
} else {
  console.log("Try again");
}

In this case, the condition is true, so the program prints Welcome.

