To understand how decisions work, consider this example:

const score = 85;
if (score === 100) {
  console.log("Perfect");
} else {
  console.log("Keep trying");
}

In this code, the program checks if the value of score is strictly equal to 100. Because 85 is not equal to 100, the condition is false, and the program skips the first block to execute the else block.

Remember that === is used to compare two values to see if they are identical. In contrast, = is used to assign a value to a binding. If you used score = 100 inside the parentheses, you would be changing the value of the score binding rather than checking it. Always look for the triple equals sign when you want to test for equality.

