To understand how functions work, consider a function that calculates a discount.

function applyDiscount(price) {
  return price - 2;
}

const finalPrice = applyDiscount(20);

In this example, 20 is the argument passed to the function. Inside, the parameter price takes the value 20. The return keyword sends the result, 18, back to the caller. The variable finalPrice then stores that 18.

If you used console.log(price - 2) instead of return, the function would display the result in the console, but the caller would receive undefined. This is because console.log is for viewing data, while return is for passing data back to the rest of your program. If a function finishes without a return statement, it automatically returns undefined.

